## Ludwig van Beethoven

{#0F766E}**Why this fits**{/}

Beethoven continued composing as his hearing deteriorated; his Ninth Symphony premiered when he was profoundly deaf.

## Charles Darwin

{#0F766E}**Why this fits**{/}

Darwin presented natural selection as the main mechanism of evolution in On the Origin of Species (1859).

## Cleopatra VII

{#0F766E}**Why this fits**{/}

The last active Ptolemaic ruler used both alliances to defend Egypt's independence as Roman power expanded.

## Qin Shi Huang

{#0F766E}**Why this fits**{/}

He became China's first emperor in 221 BCE and imposed empire-wide standards.

## Zheng He

{#0F766E}**Why this fits**{/}

His early-15th-century fleets visited ports from Southeast Asia to East Africa.

## Marie Curie

{#0F766E}**Why this fits**{/}

She won the 1903 Physics Prize and the 1911 Chemistry Prize for pioneering research on radioactivity.

## Mansa Musa

{#0F766E}**Why this fits**{/}

His 1324–25 hajj displayed Mali's wealth and strengthened connections across the Islamic world.

## Toussaint Louverture

{#0F766E}**Why this fits**{/}

His leadership helped destroy slavery in Saint-Domingue and prepare the way for independent Haiti.

## Ada Lovelace

{#0F766E}**Why this fits**{/}

Her 1843 notes described steps for calculating Bernoulli numbers and anticipated general-purpose computing.

## Rabindranath Tagore

{#0F766E}**Why this fits**{/}

The Bengali poet received the 1913 prize; his writing and songs influenced culture across South Asia.
